## What is CSS?

**CSS** stands for Cascading Style Sheets. While HTML describes the structure and meaning of a page, CSS describes its presentation.

For example, HTML can tell the browser that something is a heading:

~~~html
<h1>My profile</h1>
~~~

CSS can tell the browser how that heading should look:

~~~css
h1 {
  font-size: 2rem;
}
~~~

The basic idea is simple: CSS selects HTML elements and applies declarations to them.

## How CSS attaches to HTML

There are three common ways to put CSS on a page.

### A style element

You can place CSS inside a `<style>` element, usually in the document's `<head>`:

~~~html
<head>
  <style>
    p {
      color: navy;
    }
  </style>
</head>
~~~

This is convenient for small examples and exercises.

### An external stylesheet

For a real project, CSS is commonly stored in its own file.

For example, `styles.css` could contain:

~~~css
p {
  color: navy;
}
~~~

The HTML can load it with:

~~~html
<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">
~~~

This keeps the structure and styling separate and makes the stylesheet reusable across multiple pages.

### Inline styles

You can also put CSS directly on an element:

~~~html
<p style="color: navy;">Hello</p>
~~~

Although inline styles work, avoid using them as your normal styling strategy. They mix presentation into the HTML and make styles harder to maintain and override consistently.

Prefer classes and stylesheets for normal application development.

## CSS rules and declarations

A CSS rule has a selector followed by one or more declarations.

~~~diagram
{"type": "layers", "title": "Anatomy of a CSS rule", "layers": [{"label": "Selector", "sub": "which elements", "items": [".card h2"], "highlight": true}, {"label": "Property", "sub": "what to change", "items": ["color", "margin-bottom"]}, {"label": "Value", "sub": "to what", "items": ["#1a1a1a", "1rem"]}, {"label": "Declaration", "sub": "property: value;", "items": ["color: #1a1a1a;"]}]}
~~~

~~~css
.card {
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid black;
}
~~~

`.card` is the **selector**. It identifies which elements the rule applies to.

`padding: 20px` is a declaration. `padding` is the property and `20px` is its value.

A rule can contain many declarations:

~~~css
.card {
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid black;
  background: white;
}
~~~

The browser reads these declarations and calculates the resulting styles for matching elements.

## CSS selectors

Selectors are one of the most important parts of CSS because they determine what you are styling.

### Type selector

A type selector targets elements by their HTML element name:

~~~css
p {
  line-height: 1.6;
}
~~~

This applies to paragraphs.

### Class selector

A class selector starts with a dot:

~~~css
.card {
  padding: 20px;
}
~~~

It targets elements with `class="card"`.

Classes are usually the most useful way to create reusable styling rules.

~~~html
<article class="card">...</article>
<article class="card">...</article>
~~~

Both cards can share the same CSS.

### ID selector

An ID selector starts with `#`:

~~~css
#profile {
  max-width: 400px;
}
~~~

It targets an element with a matching `id`.

IDs have their uses, especially for uniquely identifying elements for JavaScript, links, and accessibility relationships. But they are usually not the best choice for reusable styling because they have high specificity.

Prefer classes for most CSS styling.

### Attribute selector

You can select elements based on attributes:

~~~css
input[type="email"] {
  border-color: steelblue;
}
~~~

This targets inputs whose `type` attribute is `email`.

### Descendant selector

A space between selectors means "an element inside another matching element":

~~~css
.card p {
  color: #555;
}
~~~

This selects paragraphs anywhere inside an element with the `card` class.

### Child selector

The `>` combinator selects direct children only:

~~~css
.card > p {
  margin-top: 0;
}
~~~

This matches a `p` that is directly inside `.card`, but not a paragraph nested several levels deeper.

### Pseudo-classes

A **pseudo-class** describes a state or condition.

For example:

~~~css
.button:hover {
  background: darkblue;
}
~~~

`:hover` applies when the pointer is over the element.

`:focus-visible` is especially useful for keyboard accessibility:

~~~css
.button:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid currentColor;
}
~~~

This lets you provide a clear focus indicator when an element receives keyboard focus.

### Pseudo-elements

A **pseudo-element** represents a generated part of an element rather than an actual HTML element.

For example:

~~~css
.card::before {
  content: "";
  display: block;
}
~~~

`::before` can generate content before an element's normal content. Pseudo-elements are useful for decoration and certain visual effects, but important information should normally exist in the HTML rather than only in generated CSS content.

## The cascade

The word **cascading** in CSS refers to how the browser decides which declarations win when several rules apply to the same element.

~~~diagram
{"type": "steps", "title": "Who wins the cascade?", "caption": "Checked in this order — a later step only matters when the earlier ones tie.", "steps": [{"label": "Importance", "sub": "!important beats normal"}, {"label": "Specificity", "sub": "id > class > element"}, {"label": "Source order", "sub": "the later rule wins"}, {"label": "Inheritance", "sub": "only if nothing matched"}]}
~~~

Suppose you have:

~~~css
p {
  color: black;
}

p {
  color: blue;
}
~~~

Both rules match the paragraph. The later declaration wins because the selectors have the same specificity and the second rule comes later.

The cascade considers several factors, including the origin and importance of declarations, selector specificity, and source order.

### Specificity

**Specificity** is a way of determining how strongly a selector targets an element.

As a simplified mental model, selectors have roughly this priority:

| Selector | Example | Relative specificity |
|---|---|---|
| Type | `p` | Low |
| Class / attribute / pseudo-class | `.card`, `[type="email"]`, `:hover` | Medium |
| ID | `#profile` | High |
| Inline style | `style="..."` | Very high |

For example:

~~~css
p {
  color: black;
}

.card p {
  color: blue;
}

#profile p {
  color: green;
}
~~~

If a paragraph matches all three rules and there are no other relevant factors, the ID-based selector is stronger than the class-based selector, which is stronger than the type selector.

This is why using lots of IDs and increasingly complicated selectors can make CSS difficult to maintain.

Prefer simple, purposeful classes:

~~~css
.profile-name {
  color: #222;
}
~~~

### Source order

When relevant rules have equal specificity, the later declaration usually wins.

~~~css
.button {
  background: blue;
}

.button {
  background: green;
}
~~~

The button will have a green background.

### Inheritance

Some CSS properties are inherited from a parent element by default.

For example, text colour often inherits:

~~~css
body {
  color: #222;
}
~~~

A paragraph inside the body can inherit that colour without having its own `color` declaration.

Not every property inherits. Layout properties such as `margin`, `padding`, and `border` do not normally inherit from parents.

Understanding inheritance helps explain why changing a style on a parent can affect many descendants.

### Origin and importance

CSS declarations can come from different origins, such as browser defaults, the website's own styles, and user styles. CSS also has the `!important` mechanism, which changes the normal priority rules.

As a beginner, you should avoid reaching for `!important` whenever a style does not work.

Usually, the better solution is to understand which rule is winning and adjust the selector or source order appropriately.

## The box model

One of the most important CSS concepts is the **box model**.

~~~widget box-model
~~~

Every element is treated as a box with several areas:

~~~text
┌───────────────────────────────┐
│            margin             │
│  ┌─────────────────────────┐  │
│  │         border          │  │
│  │  ┌───────────────────┐  │  │
│  │  │      padding      │  │  │
│  │  │  ┌─────────────┐  │  │  │
│  │  │  │   content   │  │  │  │
│  │  │  └─────────────┘  │  │  │
│  │  └───────────────────┘  │  │
│  └─────────────────────────┘  │
└───────────────────────────────┘
~~~

The four areas are:

- **Content** — the actual text, image, or child content.
- **Padding** — space between the content and border.
- **Border** — the visible edge around the element.
- **Margin** — space outside the element.

For example:

~~~css
.card {
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid #ccc;
  margin: 30px;
}
~~~

Padding makes the content breathe inside the card. Margin creates space between the card and other elements.

## box-sizing: border-box

By default, CSS historically uses `content-box` for the `box-sizing` property.

That means a declared width normally describes the content area, with padding and border added outside it.

For example:

~~~css
.card {
  width: 300px;
  padding: 20px;
  border: 1px solid black;
}
~~~

The rendered outer width can therefore be larger than 300px.

Many developers prefer:

~~~css
* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
~~~

With `border-box`, the declared width includes the content, padding, and border.

This makes sizing more predictable.

You can also apply it to specific elements:

~~~css
.card {
  box-sizing: border-box;
}
~~~

For modern projects, using `border-box` consistently is a common and practical convention.

## Margin collapsing

Vertical margins between normal block elements can sometimes **collapse**, meaning two adjacent vertical margins combine rather than simply being added together. For example, if one paragraph has a bottom margin of 20px and the next has a top margin of 30px, the resulting separation may be 30px rather than 50px. Margin collapsing has specific rules and does not happen in every layout context; it is mainly something to be aware of when debugging unexpected vertical spacing. Padding, borders, flexbox, and grid often provide ways to avoid these situations when you need predictable spacing.

## CSS units

CSS supports many units. You do not need to use all of them immediately.

### px

`px` is a CSS pixel unit.

~~~css
border-width: 1px;
~~~

It is useful for things where a concrete size makes sense, such as borders and some fine-grained spacing.

### rem

`rem` is relative to the root element's font size.

~~~css
padding: 1rem;
font-size: 1.5rem;
~~~

It is often a good choice for typography and spacing because it scales consistently with the root font size.

### em

`em` is relative to the font size of the relevant element, which can make it behave differently depending on where it is used.

~~~css
.button {
  padding: 0.5em 1em;
}
~~~

It can be useful when you want spacing to scale with the element's own text size.

### Percentages

Percentages are commonly relative to a containing value.

~~~css
.card {
  width: 80%;
}
~~~

They are useful for fluid layouts.

### vw

`vw` means viewport width. `1vw` represents one percent of the viewport width.

~~~css
.hero-title {
  font-size: 5vw;
}
~~~

Viewport units can be useful for responsive designs, although using them directly for all text can produce impractical sizes. Combining units with functions such as `clamp()` is often better for responsive typography.

A useful starting rule is:

~~~text
px  → precise small details
rem → typography and consistent spacing
em  → sizing relative to an element's text
%   → fluid sizes relative to a containing context
vw  → sizes related to viewport width
~~~

## Colours

CSS supports several ways of describing colours.

### Hexadecimal

A hexadecimal colour uses six hexadecimal digits:

~~~css
color: #1f2937;
background: #ffffff;
~~~

You will see hex colours frequently in CSS.

### RGB

RGB describes red, green, and blue components:

~~~css
color: rgb(31, 41, 55);
~~~

You can also use modern colour syntax with transparency when needed.

### HSL

HSL stands for Hue, Saturation, and Lightness:

~~~css
color: hsl(220 15% 20%);
~~~

HSL can be useful when you want to reason about changing the lightness or saturation of a colour.

There is no universal rule saying one colour notation is always best. Pick a consistent style for your project.

## CSS custom properties

CSS **custom properties**, often called CSS variables, let you define reusable values.

For example:

~~~css
:root {
  --accent: #2563eb;
  --text: #222;
}

.button {
  background-color: var(--accent);
  color: white;
}
~~~

`:root` represents the document's root element and is a common place to define global variables.

The value can then be used with `var()`.

~~~css
.card {
  color: var(--text);
}
~~~

This becomes particularly useful when a project has a design system with repeated colours, spacing values, or other settings.

You can also provide a fallback:

~~~css
color: var(--text, #222);
~~~

If `--text` is unavailable, the fallback value is used.

## Reading CSS in DevTools

When CSS does not behave as expected, do not guess. Open your browser's Developer Tools and inspect the element.

~~~callout tip Let DevTools explain the cascade
Right-click an element → **Inspect**. The **Styles** pane lists every matching rule, most specific first, with losing declarations struck through. The **Computed** tab shows the final value and lets you jump to the rule that set it.
~~~

The **Styles** pane shows the CSS rules that apply to the selected element.

You may see rules crossed out. A crossed-out declaration usually means another declaration wins according to the cascade.

For example:

~~~css
.card {
  color: red;
}

.profile-card {
  color: blue;
}
~~~

If the element has both classes, you can inspect which rule wins.

The **Computed** pane shows the final calculated values after the browser has resolved the cascade, inheritance, variables, and other CSS rules.

This distinction is useful:

~~~text
Styles   → Which rules are trying to style this element?
Computed → What value did the browser finally calculate?
~~~

If you want to understand why `padding` is not what you expected, the Computed pane can show the final padding values. The Styles pane can help you discover which declaration produced them.

DevTools is not just for fixing broken CSS. It is a way to learn how the browser interprets your code.

## A practical styling example

Imagine this HTML:

~~~html
<article class="card">
  <h2>David Mészáros</h2>
  <p class="role">Senior Full-Stack Developer</p>
  <a class="button" href="/contact">Contact me</a>
</article>
~~~

A simple stylesheet could be:

~~~css
:root {
  --accent: #2563eb;
}

.card {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  max-width: 400px;
  padding: 24px;
  border: 1px solid #d1d5db;
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.card h2 {
  margin-top: 0;
}

.role {
  color: #555;
}

.button {
  display: inline-block;
  padding: 10px 16px;
  background-color: var(--accent);
  color: white;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.button:hover {
  opacity: 0.9;
}

.button:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid currentColor;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}
~~~

Notice that the styles use classes for reusable concepts, a custom property for the accent colour, and `border-box` for predictable sizing.

## Avoid fighting the cascade

When CSS becomes difficult, beginners often respond by writing increasingly complicated selectors:

~~~css
main div section article div.card a.button span {
  color: red;
}
~~~

This may work temporarily, but it creates a fragile dependency on the exact HTML structure.

Prefer a simple class when possible:

~~~css
.button-label {
  color: red;
}
~~~

Simple selectors are easier to understand, reuse, and override.

The goal is not to win a specificity battle. The goal is to make the stylesheet predictable.

## A CSS debugging checklist

When a style does not work, check these questions in order:

1. **Does the selector actually match the element?**
2. **Is the stylesheet loaded?**
3. **Is another rule winning in the cascade?**
4. **Is the value inherited from a parent?**
5. **Is the box model producing a different size than expected?**
6. **Is a CSS variable defined and resolving to the value you expect?**
7. **What does the Computed pane show?**

This is much faster than randomly adding `!important`, changing values, and hoping the result looks right.

## The core CSS mental model

You can understand most beginner CSS problems by remembering four ideas.

**Selectors** decide what an element should be styled by.

**The cascade** decides which matching declarations win.

**The box model** determines how an element's content, padding, border, and margin contribute to its size and spacing.

**Inheritance and custom properties** let values flow through the document and be reused consistently.

Once these concepts make sense, CSS stops feeling like a collection of mysterious rules. You can inspect an element, identify the matching selectors, understand the winning declaration, and reason about its final size and appearance.

That is the foundation you need before moving into Flexbox, Grid, and responsive layouts.
